--TEST--
`int`, `uint`, `float` and `decimal` are one numeric domain wherever equality is resolved at runtime — a `mixed` `==` and `Core\Arr`'s strict identity alike — ADR 0090 §§ 3, 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5: a `mixed` operand resolves on the runtime tags and applies § 3's
// table, whose numeric row is *one* row — so this is `true` where PHP's
// `1 === 1.0` was `false`.
mixed $n = 1;
mixed $f = 1.0;
decimal $scaled = 1.00;
mixed $d = $scaled;
echo ($n == $f) as string, "|", ($d == $n) as string, "|", ($d == $f) as string, "|\n";

// …and the same row still separates two different numbers, and still answers
// `false` rather than throwing when the two tags name different rows.
mixed $half = 1.5;
mixed $text = "1";
echo ($n == $half) as string, "|", ($n == $text) as string, "|\n";

// `Core\Arr`'s "strict identity" is that same comparison, so a needle matches
// across representations too — one comparison with two answers would be the
// worse trap.
array<mixed> $holds = [1.0, "x"];
echo Core\Arr::contains($holds, 1) as string, "|", Core\Arr::contains($holds, 2) as string, "|\n";

// `unique` indexes that comparison with a hash, so the hash has to agree with
// it: `1`, `1.0` and `1.00` are one value and collapse to one entry.
array<mixed> $numbers = [1, 1.0, $scaled, 2];
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Arr::unique($numbers)) as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
1|1|1|
||
1||
2
